---
tracker:
  kind: linear
  project_slug: MY-PROJECT-SLUG
  api_key: $LINEAR_API_KEY
  active_states:
    - Todo
    - In Progress
  terminal_states:
    - Done
    - Closed
    - Cancelled
    - Canceled
    - Duplicate

polling:
  interval_ms: 30000

workspace:
  root: ~/symphony_workspaces

hooks:
  after_create: |
    git clone https://github.com/singh-krishan/claude-symphony.git .
    npm install
  before_run: |
    git checkout main && git pull
    BRANCH="symphony/$(echo '{{issue.identifier}}' | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
    git checkout -b "$BRANCH" 2>/dev/null || git checkout "$BRANCH"
  after_run: |
    git add -A
    git commit -m "{{issue.identifier}}: {{issue.title}}" --allow-empty || true
    BRANCH="symphony/$(echo '{{issue.identifier}}' | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
    git push -u origin "$BRANCH" --force-with-lease
    if ! gh pr list --head "$BRANCH" --json number --jq '.[0].number' | grep -q .; then
      gh pr create \
        --title "{{issue.identifier}}: {{issue.title}}" \
        --body "$(cat <<BODY
## Linear Issue
[{{issue.identifier}}]({{issue.url}})

## Description
{{issue.description}}

---
Automated by Claude Symphony
BODY
)" \
        --head "$BRANCH"
    fi
  before_remove: |
    BRANCH="symphony/$(echo '{{issue.identifier}}' | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
    git push origin --delete "$BRANCH" 2>/dev/null || true
  timeout_ms: 120000

agent:
  max_concurrent_agents: 3
  max_turns: 20
  max_retry_backoff_ms: 300000

claude:
  model: claude-sonnet-4-6
  max_tokens: 8096
---

You are a senior software engineer working autonomously on a production codebase.

## Your Task
**{{issue.identifier}}: {{issue.title}}**

{% if issue.description %}
### Description
{{issue.description}}
{% endif %}

{% if issue.labels.size > 0 %}
### Labels
{% for label in issue.labels %}- {{label}}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if issue.priority %}
### Priority: {{issue.priority}} (1=urgent, 4=low)
{% endif %}

## Workflow
1. Start by reading the codebase to understand the architecture.
   - Run `find . -type f -name "*.ts" | head -30` to see the file structure.
   - Read key files like package.json, tsconfig.json, and entry points.
2. Plan your approach before writing any code.
3. Implement the fix or feature described in the issue.
4. Write or update tests if the project has a test framework set up.
5. Run `npm run build` to verify the code compiles without errors.
6. If tests exist, run them and make sure they pass.

## Rules
- Keep changes focused — only modify what the issue requires.
- Do NOT modify WORKFLOW.md, WORKFLOW.*.example, or issues.json.
- Follow existing code conventions (naming, formatting, patterns).
- If the issue is ambiguous, make a reasonable decision and document it
  with a brief code comment explaining your choice.

{% if attempt %}
**This is retry attempt #{{attempt}}.**
A previous attempt failed. Check what exists in the workspace:
- Run `git log --oneline -5` to see previous commits.
- Run `git diff HEAD~1` to see what changed last time.
- Fix the issue that caused the failure and continue.
{% endif %}
